@node Sync direction (one-way sync)
@section Sync direction (one-way sync)

By default@comma{} org-caldav does two-way syncing@comma{} that means it does not
matter where and how you change an entry. You can also move Org
entries freely from one file to another@comma{} as long as they are all
listed in @code{org-caldav-files}.

To do one-way sync only@comma{} set @code{org-caldav-sync-direction} to
@code{'org->cal} or @code{'cal->org}@comma{} depending on which direction you'd like to
have. If you choose @code{'org->cal}@comma{} then @code{org-caldav-inbox} won't matter
and can be @code{nil}. Likewise@comma{} if you choose @code{'cal->org}@comma{} then
@code{org-caldav-files} will be ignored and only the calendar will be
imported into the inbox.

WARNING: It is NOT safe to switch the same calendar between 1-way and
2-way sync modes.  Doing so may cause unexpected behavior@comma{} such as
deleting all events in the calendar.

@node Filtering entries
@section Filtering entries

There are several possibilities to choose which entries should be
synced and which not:

@itemize
@item
If you only want to sync manually marked entries@comma{} use
@code{org-caldav-select-tags}@comma{} which is directly mapped to
@code{org-export-select-tags}@comma{} so see its doc-string on how it works.

@item
If you want to exclude certain tags@comma{} use @code{org-caldav-exclude-tags}@comma{}
which is mapped to @code{org-icalendar-exclude} tags.

@item
If you want more fine grained control@comma{} use
@code{org-caldav-skip-conditions}. The syntax of the conditions is
described in the doc-string of @code{org-agenda-skip-if}.

@item
In case you just want to keep your remote calendar clean@comma{} set
@code{org-caldav-days-in-past} to the number of days you want to keep in
the past on the remote calendar. This does not affect your org files@comma{}
it works just as a filter for entries older than N days.
@end itemize

Note however that the normal @code{org-agenda-skip-function(-global)} will
@strong{not} have any effect on the icalendar exporter (this used to be the
case@comma{} but changed with the new exporters).

@node Syncing deletions
@section Syncing deletions

If you delete entries in your Org files@comma{} the corresponding iCalendar
entries will by default get deleted. You can change that behavior with
@code{org-caldav-delete-calendar-entries} to never delete@comma{} or to ask before
deletion.

You must be careful to not simply remove previously synced files from
@code{org-caldav-files}@comma{} as org-caldav would view all the entries from those
files as deleted and hence by default also delete them from the
calendar.  However@comma{} org-caldav should be able to detect this situation
and warn you with the message 'Previously synced file(s) are missing'@comma{}
asking you whether to continue nonetheless.

If you delete events in your calendar@comma{} you will by default get asked
if you'd like to delete the corresponding Org event. You can change
that behavior through @code{org-caldav-delete-org-entries}.

If you answer a deletion request with "no"@comma{} the event should get
re-synced to the calendar next time you call @code{org-caldav-sync}.

@node Storing authentication information in authinfo/netrc
@section Storing authentication information in authinfo/netrc

If you don't want to enter your user/password every time@comma{} you can
store it in an authinfo file@comma{} so that the auth-source package will
take care of the login automatically.  For more information@comma{} see
@code{(info "auth")}@comma{} especially section "Help for users".

In Emacs 31+@comma{} you can use a line like the following:

@example
machine www.example.com port https login MYLOGINNAME password MYPASSWORD
@end example

In Emacs 30 or earlier@comma{} the line must also specify the port number in the URL:

@example
machine www.example.com:443 port https login username password secret
@end example

For more information about the syntax change in Emacs 31@comma{} see @uref{https://github.com/dengste/org-caldav/issues/320, Issue #320}.

Since you are storing your password in a file you should encrypt it
using GnuPG@. Emacs will prompt you for a decryption key when it tries
to read the file.

@node Syncing with more than one calendar
@section Syncing with more than one calendar

This can be done by setting the variable @code{org-caldav-calendars}. It
should be a list of plists (a 'plist' is simply a list with alternating
:key's and values). Through these plists@comma{} you can override the global
values of variables like @code{org-caldav-calendar-id}@comma{} and calling
@code{org-caldav-sync} will go through these plists in order.

Example:

@lisp
(setq org-caldav-calendars
  '((:calendar-id "work@@whatever" :files ("~/org/work.org")
     :inbox "~/org/fromwork.org")
    (:calendar-id "stuff@@mystuff"
     :files ("~/org/sports.org" "~/org/play.org")
     :skip-conditions (regexp "soccer")
     :inbox "~/org/fromstuff.org")) )
@end lisp

This means that you have two calendars with IDs "work@@whatever" and
"stuff@@mystuff". Both will be accessed through the global value of
org-caldav-url@comma{} since the key :url isn't specified. The calendar
"work@@whatever" will be synced with the file 'work.org' and inbox
'fromwork.org'@comma{} while "stuff@@mystuff" with 'sports.org' and
'play.org'@comma{} @emph{unless} there's the string 'soccer' in the heading@comma{} and
and inbox is 'fromstuff.org'. See the doc-string of
@code{org-caldav-calendars} for more details on which keys you can use.

@node Customizing the inbox
@section Customizing the inbox

See the doc-string of @code{org-caldav-inbox} if you want more flexibility in
where new items should be put. Instead of simply providing a file@comma{} you
can also choose an existing entry or headline@comma{} or put the entry under a
datetree.

@node Syncing TODOs between Org and CalDav
@section Syncing TODOs between Org and CalDav

This feature is relatively new and less well tested@comma{} so it is
recommended to have backups before using it.  It has been tested on
nextcloud and radicale.

To sync TODO's between Org and the CalDav server@comma{} do:

@lisp
(setq org-icalendar-include-todo 'all
    org-caldav-sync-todo t)
@end lisp

The first instructs the Org exporter to include TODOs; the second
tells org-caldav to import icalendar VTODOs as Org TODOs.

Other customizations to consider (see their documentation for more
details):

@itemize
@item
@code{org-caldav-todo-priority} to control how priority levels map between
iCalendar and Org.
@item
@code{org-caldav-todo-percent-states} to convert between
@code{org-todo-keywords} and iCalendar's percent-complete property.
@item
@code{org-caldav-todo-deadline-schedule-warning-days} to auto-create
SCHEDULED timestamps when a DEADLINE is present (this might be useful
for users of the OpenTasks app).
@end itemize

If you find that some Org entries get an extra tag which equals their
CATEGORY@comma{} this might be caused by the CATEGORY being exported to
iCalendar@comma{} and then re-imported to Org as a tag. In that case@comma{} do

@lisp
(setq org-icalendar-categories '(local-tags))
@end lisp

to prevent the CATEGORY from being exported to iCalendar. This problem
only seems to affect some CalDav servers: in particular@comma{} NextCloud
is affected@comma{} but Radicale does not seem to experience this problem.

@node Behavior of recurring TODO deadlines without a start time
@section Behavior of recurring TODO deadlines without a start time

Technically@comma{} the @uref{https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5545#section-3.8.2.4, iCalendar spec} requires repeating events and todos
(i.e. having an @code{RRULE} property) to have a starting time (@code{DTSTART}
in iCalendar@comma{} equivalent to @code{SCHEDULED} in Org TODOs).  This means
that@comma{} a TODO with a repeating @code{DEADLINE} but without a @code{SCHEDULED}
property@comma{} such as below@comma{} is not allowed by the iCalendar spec:

@example
* TODO An example todo with a repeating deadline and no start time
DEADLINE: <2024-09-15 Sun +1w>
@end example

This is a clear shortcoming of the iCalendar spec@comma{} because it @emph{does}
allow tasks to have a standalone deadline without a starting time@comma{} but
@emph{doesn't} allow such tasks to repeat.

By default@comma{} @code{ox-icalendar} follows the iCalendar spec@comma{} and when
exporting a TODO with a repeating @code{DEADLINE} but no @code{SCHEDULED}
timestamp@comma{} will add a start time based on
@code{org-deadline-warning-days}. On future syncs@comma{} this start time will be
inserted into Org as a @code{SCHEDULED} timestamp.

However@comma{} in practice@comma{} many iCalendar implementations ignore this
limitation@comma{} and allow Todos with @code{DEADLINE} (@code{DUE}) times to have
repeaters (@code{RRULE})@comma{} even if they are missing @code{SCHEDULED} (@code{DTSTART})
times. If your CalDav server allows this@comma{} then you may set the
variable @code{org-icalendar-todo-unscheduled-start} to @code{nil}.  This will
prevent @code{ox-icalendar} from adding a start time to such TODOs@comma{} thus
preventing the @code{SCHEDULED} timestamp from being inserted on future
syncs.

See @ref{Repeating events and todos, , Repeating events and todos} for more details about how org-caldav
handles repeating events and todos.

@node Compatible CalDav servers
@chapter Compatible CalDav servers

@itemize
@item
@strong{Owncloud} and @strong{Nextcloud}: Regularly tested.

@item
@strong{Radicale} and @strong{Baikal}: Works. If you get problems with 'Digest'
authentication@comma{} switch back to 'Basic' (make sure to use https@comma{}
though!). If you get asked for password repeatedly@comma{} put it in
@code{.authinfo} file (@ref{Storing authentication information in authinfo/netrc, , Storing authentication information}).

@item
@strong{SOGo} and @strong{Kolab}: Reported to be working
(@uref{https://docs.kolab.org/client-configuration/emacs.html})

@item
@strong{Google Calendar}: Should work@comma{} but you need to register an
application with the Google Developer Console for OAuth2
authentication (see below)@comma{} because Google explicitly forbids to put
client id/secrets into open source software (see
@uref{https://developers.google.com/terms}@comma{} section 4b@comma{} paragraph 1). Instead
of doing that though@comma{} I'd rather suggest you choose another service
provider.
@end itemize

