@node Syncing to Google Calendar
@section Syncing to Google Calendar

NOTE: Using org-caldav with Google Calendar may be currently
broken. See @uref{https://github.com/dengste/org-caldav/issues/284}

The CalDAV endpoint for Google Calendar requires OAuth2
authentication.  So first@comma{} you need to install the oauth2 library from
GNU ELPA@comma{} and afterwards you need to acquire an application ID and
secret from the Google Developer Console. For details on how to do
this@comma{} follow the Google documentation at

@uref{https://developers.google.com/google-apps/calendar/caldav/v2/guide#creating_your_client_id}

Put the client ID and secret into @code{org-caldav-oauth2-client-id} and
@code{org-caldav-oauth2-client-secret}@comma{} respectively. Then set
@code{org-caldav-url} to the symbol @code{'google}@comma{} and look up the
@code{org-caldav-calendar-id} as described above.

On first connection@comma{} the oauth2 library should redirect you to the
Google OAuth2 authentication site. This requires a javascript enabled
browser@comma{} so make sure that @code{browse-url-browser-function} is set to
something like @code{browse-url-firefox} (the internal eww or w3m browsers
will @strong{not} work). After authentication@comma{} you will be given a key that
you have to paste into the Emacs prompt. The oauth2 library will save
this key in Emacs' secure plist store@comma{} which is encrypted with
GnuPG@. If you have not yet used a secure plist store@comma{} you will be
asked for its encryption passphrase. In the future@comma{} you should only
need to enter that passphrase again to connect with Google Calendar.

By default@comma{} plstore will @strong{not} cache your entered password@comma{} so it will
possibly ask you @strong{many} times. To activate caching@comma{} use

@lisp
(setq plstore-cache-passphrase-for-symmetric-encryption t)
@end lisp

