@node Org and the iCalendar format
@section Org and the iCalendar format

An Org entry can store much more information than an iCalendar entry@comma{}
so there is no one-to-one correspondence between the two formats which
makes syncing a bit difficult.

@itemize
@item
Org to iCalendar
@end itemize

This package uses the org-icalendar package to do the export to the
iCalendar format (.ics files). By default@comma{} it uses the title of the
Org entry as SUMMARY and puts the entry's body into DESCRIPTION@comma{}
snipping stuff like properties and timestamps (you can override that
with properties of the same name@comma{} but IMO it makes stuff just more
complicated). The variable @code{org-icalendar-include-body}
denotes how many characters from the body should be included as
DESCRIPTION (by default all characters are included).

@itemize
@item
iCalendar to Org
@end itemize

If you create a new iCalendar entry in your calendar@comma{} you'll get an
Org entry with SUMMARY as heading@comma{} DESCRIPTION as body and the
timestamp. However@comma{} if you @emph{change} an existing entry in the calendar@comma{}
things get more complicated and the variable
@code{org-caldav-sync-changes-to-org} comes into play. Its default is the
symbol "title-and-timestamp"@comma{} which means that only the entry's
heading is synced (with SUMMARY) and the timestamp gets updated@comma{} but
@emph{not} the entry's body with DESCRIPTION@.  The simple reason is that
you might loose data@comma{} since DESCRIPTION is rather limited in what it
can store. Still@comma{} you can set the variable to the symbol "all"@comma{} which
will completely @emph{replace} an existing Org entry with the entry that
gets generated from the calendar's event. You can also limit syncing
to heading and/or timestamp only.

To be extra safe@comma{} org-caldav will by default backup entries it
changes. See the variable @code{org-caldav-backup-file} for details.

@itemize
@item
Org sexp entries
@end itemize

A special case are sexp entries like

@example
%%(diary-anniversary  2 2 1969) Foo's birthday

* Regular meeting
  <%%(diary-float t 4 2)>
@end example

As you can see@comma{} they can appear in two different ways: plain by
themselves@comma{} or inside an Org entry. If they are inside an Org entry@comma{}
there's a good chance they will be exported (see below) and have an ID
property@comma{} so they can be found by org-caldav. We can sync the title@comma{}
but syncing the timestamp with the s-expression is just infeasible@comma{} so
this will generate a sync error (which are @emph{not} critical; you'll just
see them at the end of the sync@comma{} just so that you're aware that some
stuff wasn't synced properly).

However@comma{} sexp-entries are insanely flexible@comma{} and there are limits as
to what the icalendar exporter will handle. For example@comma{} this here

@example
** Regular event
   <%%(memq (calendar-day-of-week date) '(1 3 5))>
@end example

will not be exported at all.

If the sexp entry is not inside an Org entry but stands by itself@comma{}
they still will be exported@comma{} but they won't get an ID (since IDs are
properties linked to Org entries). In practice@comma{} that means that you
can delete and change them inside Org and this will be synced@comma{} but if
you @emph{change} them in the @emph{calendar}@comma{} this will @emph{not} get synced
back. Org-caldav just cannot find those entries@comma{} so this will generate
a one-time sync error instead (again: those are not critical@comma{} just
FYI). If you don't want those entries to be exported at all@comma{} just set
@code{org-icalendar-include-sexps} to nil.

@node Conflict handling
@section Conflict handling

Now that's an easy one: Org always wins. That means@comma{} if you change an
entry in Org @emph{and} in the calendar@comma{} the changes in the calendar will
be lost. I might implement proper conflict handling some day@comma{} but
don't hold your breath (patches are welcome@comma{} of course).

@node Repeating events and todos
@section Repeating events and todos

Org-caldav has basic support for repeating events and todos. In
particular@comma{} simple Org timestamp repeaters such as @code{+3d} or @code{+1m} can
be succesfully sync'd bidirectionally.

However@comma{} complex iCalender recurrences@comma{} such as "repeat on the 2nd
Tuesday of each month until X date"@comma{} are not supported.

For Org TODOs with both @code{SCHEDULED} and @code{DEADLINE} timestamps@comma{} each of
the timestamps must have the same repeater@comma{} otherwise the behavior is
undefined.

Furthermore@comma{} the behavior of Org TODOs with a repeating @code{DEADLINE}
timestamp@comma{} but no @code{SCHEDULED} timestamp@comma{} has some subtleties; see the
configuration section: @ref{Behavior of recurring TODO deadlines without a start time, , Behavior of recurring TODO deadlines without a start time}.

@node How syncing happens (aka David's little CalDAV rant)
@section How syncing happens (a.k.a. David's little CalDAV rant)

(This is probably not interesting@comma{} so you can skip this.)

CalDAV is a mess.

First off@comma{} it is based on WebDAV@comma{} which has its own fair share of
problems. The main design flaw of CalDAV however@comma{} is that UID and
resource name (the "filename"@comma{} if you want) are two different
things. I know that there are reasons for that (not everything has a
UID@comma{} like timezones@comma{} and you can put several events in one resource)@comma{}
but this is typical over-engineering to allow some marginal use cases
pretty much no one needs. Another problem is that you have to do
additional round-trips to get Etag and sequence number@comma{} which makes
CalDAV pretty slow.

Org-caldav takes the easy route: it assumes that every resource
contains one event@comma{} and that UID and resource name are identical. In
fact@comma{} Google's CalDAV interface even enforces the latter. And while
Owncloud does not enforce it@comma{} at least it just does it if you create
items in its web interface.

However@comma{} the CalDAV standard does not demand this@comma{} so I guess there
are servers out there with which org-caldav does not work. Patches
welcome.

Now@comma{} all this would be bad enough if it weren't for the sloppy server
implementations which make implementing a CalDAV client a living hell
and led to several rewrites of the code. Especially Google@comma{} the 500
pound gorilla in the room@comma{} doesn't really care much for CalDAV@. I
guess they really like their own shiny REST-based calendar API better@comma{}
and I can't blame them for that.

@node Tips Tricks and Troubleshooting
